LLM does not directly process human readable sentences. It processes numerical representations of tokens.\n\nTokenization is the process of breaking text into smaller units called tokens.\n\nFor example, consider this sentence:\n\n```\nThe cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n```\n\nA simple word-based tokenizer might produce:\n\n```\n['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n```\n\nAnother tokenizer might produce:\n\n```\n['The', ' cat', ' sat', ' on', ' the', ' mat', ' because', ' it', ' was', ' tired', '.']\n```\n\nNotice that the second tokenizer preserves spaces as part of tokens.\n\nA subword tokenizer might produce something like this.\n\nFor another sentence:\n\n```\nTokenization is important.\n```\n\nA tokenizer could produce:\n\n```\n[\"Token\", \"ization\", \" is\", \" important\", \".\"]\n```\n\nHere, `Tokenization` is divided into two pieces:\n\n- `Token`\n- `ization`\n\nWhy?\n\n> Because modern LLMs commonly use subword tokenization. They don't necessarily need a separate vocabulary entry for every possible word.\n\n**Is tokenization case-sensitive?**\n\n| Tokenizer | Case-sensitive? |\n|---|---|\n| bert-base-uncased | No |\n| bert-base-cased | Yes |\n| GPT-2 | Yes |\n\n## Why do we need tokenization?\n\nImagine you send this prompt to an LLM:\n\n```\nThe cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n```\n\nA computer does not inherently understand the meaning of this English sentence.\n\nIt needs a numerical representation.\n\nThe process looks like this:\n\n1. Human-readable input: `The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.`\n2. Tokenization\n    - Tokens: `['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']`\n3. Token-to-ID conversion\n    - Token IDs: `[22, 24, 32, 31, 28, 25, 50, 30, 29, 34, 1]`\n4. Embedding layer\n5. Numerical vectors: Each token ID is mapped to an embedding vector.\n6. Transformer model: Attention, neural network layers, and prediction.\n\nWhy is this useful?\n\n1. Computers can efficiently process numerical data.\n2. Tokenization creates a manageable vocabulary.\n3. Subword tokenization handles unfamiliar words.\n4. It allows the model to learn patterns between language units.\n5. It makes language processing compatible with neural networks.\n\n## Different types of tokenization\n\nThere are several tokenization techniques. Let's understand each one.\n\n### Word based tokenization\n\nThis method divides text into individual words.\n","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"text = \"The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\"","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.468129Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.468445Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.488409Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.468407Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.487509Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":106},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import nltk\n\nnltk.download('punkt')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.489518Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.489893Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.513057Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.489855Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.512074Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"[nltk_data] Downloading package punkt to /usr/share/nltk_data...\n[nltk_data]   Package punkt is already up-to-date!\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":107,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"True"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":107},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\n\nword_tokens = word_tokenize(text)\nprint(word_tokens)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.515149Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.515522Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.530081Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.515494Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.529113Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":108},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**How does it work?**\n\nThe tokenizer identifies spaces and punctuation and splits the sentence accordingly.\n\n**Advantages:**\n\n- Easy to implement.\n- Easy to understand.\n- Words remain meaningful units.\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n\n- Very large vocabulary.\n- Cannot efficiently handle unseen words.\n- Different word forms may require separate vocabulary entries.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nplay\nplaying\nplayed\nplayer\n```\n\nA word-based tokenizer may need four separate vocabulary entries. This becomes problematic when working with millions of possible words.\n\n### Character-based tokenization\n\nInstead of splitting text into words, this method splits it into individual characters.","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.191621Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.192008Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.200014Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.19198Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-05T12:49:35.198793Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"char_tokens = list(text)\nprint(char_tokens)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.531154Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.531489Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.549406Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.531463Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.548344Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"['T', 'h', 'e', ' ', 'c', 'a', 't', ' ', 's', 'a', 't', ' ', 'o', 'n', ' ', 't', 'h', 'e', ' ', 'm', 'a', 't', ' ', 'b', 'e', 'c', 'a', 'u', 's', 'e', ' ', 'i', 't', ' ', 'w', 'a', 's', ' ', 't', 'i', 'r', 'e', 'd', '.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":109},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Advantages:**\n\n- Very small vocabulary.\n- Can represent almost any word.\n- Handles unfamiliar words naturally.\n\n**Disadvantages:**\n\n- Creates many more tokens.\n- The model needs to learn word-level patterns from individual characters.\n- Longer sequences increase computational costs.\n\n### Byte-based tokenization\n\nByte-based tokenization represents text using bytes, often UTF-8 bytes.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"byete_tokens = text.encode('utf-8')\n\nprint(\"Original Text:\", text)\nprint(\"Byte Tokens:\", byete_tokens)\nprint(\"Byte Values:\", list(byete_tokens))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.550812Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.551196Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.566989Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.551158Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.566001Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Original Text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\nByte Tokens: b'The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.'\nByte Values: [84, 104, 101, 32, 99, 97, 116, 32, 115, 97, 116, 32, 111, 110, 32, 116, 104, 101, 32, 109, 97, 116, 32, 98, 101, 99, 97, 117, 115, 101, 32, 105, 116, 32, 119, 97, 115, 32, 116, 105, 114, 101, 100, 46]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":110},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"A byte-level tokenizer can use individual bytes or merge frequently occurring byte sequences into larger tokens.\n\nThis is particularly useful for handling:\n\n- Unusual words.\n- Multiple languages.\n- Emojis.\n- Special characters.\n- Previously unseen text.\n\nMany modern LLM tokenizers use byte-level techniques combined with subword merging.\n\n### Subword Tokenization\n\nVery important for LLMs. Subword tokenization divides text into meaningful or frequently occurring smaller pieces.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from tokenizers import Tokenizer\nfrom tokenizers.models import BPE\nfrom tokenizers.trainers import BpeTrainer\nfrom tokenizers.pre_tokenizers import Whitespace\n\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(BPE(unk_token=\"[UNK]\"))\n\ntrainer = BpeTrainer(\n    vocab_size=100,\n    special_tokens=[\"[UNK]\"]\n)\n\ntokenizer.pre_tokenizer = Whitespace()\n\ntexts = [\n    \"The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\",\n    \"The cat was tired after sitting on the mat.\",\n    \"The mat was where the cat sat.\"\n]\n\ntokenizer.train_from_iterator(texts, trainer)\n\noutput = tokenizer.encode(text)\n\n# Display results\nprint(\"Encoder:\", output)\nprint(\"\\nOriginal text:\", text)\nprint(\"Tokens:\", output.tokens)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.61746Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.617812Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.627782Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.617785Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.626793Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\n\n\nEncoder: Encoding(num_tokens=11, attributes=[ids, type_ids, tokens, offsets, attention_mask, special_tokens_mask, overflowing])\n\nOriginal text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\nTokens: ['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":111},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The exact divisions depend on the tokenizer.\n\nWhy is subword tokenization so popular?\n\nConsider these words:\n\n```\nplay\nplaying\nplayed\nplayer\nplayful\n```\n\nA subword tokenizer can reuse the common piece `play` instead of requiring an entirely independent vocabulary entry for every variation. This provides a balance between word-based and character-based tokenization.\n\n## How do modern LLMs tokenize text internally\n\nNow let's understand three important algorithms used in modern tokenizers.\n\n### BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)\n\nImportant for LLM interviews. It is a subword tokenization algorithm that builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent units.\n\nLet's understand it step by step.\n\nSuppose our training dataset contains:\n\n```\nlow\nlower\nlowest\nnewer\nnewest\n```\n\nStep 1: Initially, divide words into characters.\n\n```\nlow   → l o w\nlower → l o w e r\nlowest → l o w e s t\nnewer → n e w e r\nnewest → n e w e s t\n```\n\nStep 2: Count frequently occurring adjacent pairs.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nl + o\no + w\ne + r\ne + s\ns + t\n```\n\nSuppose `l + o` occurs most frequently.\n\nStep 3: Merge the most frequent pair.\n\n```\nl + o → lo\n```\n\nNow the vocabulary contains `lo` as a new token.\n\nStep 4: Repeat the process.\n\nSuppose `lo + w` is now the most frequent pair.\n\n```\nlo + w → low\n```\n\nThen another merge might be:\n\n```\ne + r → er\n```\n\nThe algorithm keeps merging frequently occurring pairs until it reaches its target vocabulary size or another stopping condition.\n\nStep 5: Final tokenization\n\nThe vocabulary may now contain:\n\n```\nl\no\nw\nlo\nlow\ner\nest\nnew\n...\n```\n\nSo the word:\n\n```\nlowest\n```\n\nmight be tokenized as:\n\n```\n[\"low\", \"est\"]\n```\n\nThis is a simplified demonstration. Actual BPE tokenization depends on the learned merge rules, initial alphabet, preprocessing, and tokenization configuration.\n\n**Where is BPE used?**\n\n- GPT-family tokenizers commonly use BPE variants.\n- Many code and text language models use BPE-based tokenization.\n\n\n**Why do we need `texts`?**\n\nBecause a BPE tokenizer needs examples to learn how to split text into tokens. `texts` is your training dataset. It contains example sentences that the tokenizer learns from. Think of `texts` as the textbook from which the tokenizer learns its vocabulary.\n\nThe tokenizer analyzes these sentences to learn:\n\n- Which words appear frequently.\n- Which characters or groups of characters occur together.\n- Which subword units can be combined.\n- How to build its vocabulary.\n\n**What is the difference between training and encoding?**\n\nThis is the most important concept.\n\n| Operation | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `texts` | Provides training examples |\n| `tokenizer.train_from_iterator()` | Learns the vocabulary |\n| `tokenizer.encode()` | Converts new text into tokens |\n| `output.tokens` | Displays the resulting tokens |\n| `output.ids` | Displays numerical IDs for tokens |\n\n**Why do we encode text?**\n\nComputers and machine learning models generally work with numerical representations rather than raw text.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nThe cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n```\n\nMight become:\n\n```python\n[22, 24, 32, 31, 28, 25, 50, 30, 29, 34, 1]\n```\n\nThese numbers are called token IDs.\n\nTraining builds the tokenizer's vocabulary, while encoding uses that vocabulary to convert sentences into numbers.\n\n### WordPiece\n\nIt is associated with BERT and related models. Unlike basic BPE, WordPiece typically chooses merges based on how well they improve the likelihood of the training data under its vocabulary model.\n\n```\nplaying\n```\n\nCould be divided into:\n\n```\n[\"play\", \"##ing\"]\n````\n\n**What does `##` mean?**\n\nIt indicates that the subword is attached to the preceding token rather than beginning a new word.\n\n- For example: `unbelievable`\n- Might become: `[\"un\", \"##believ\", \"##able\"]`\n\nThe exact segmentation depends on the trained vocabulary.\n\n**Key Difference**\n\n- BPE: Uses frequency-based pair merging.\n- WordPiece: Uses a likelihood-based criterion for selecting vocabulary pieces.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\nword_piece_tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    \"bert-base-uncased\"\n)\n\nword_piece_tokens = word_piece_tokenizer.tokenize(text)\n\nprint(\"\\nOriginal text:\", text)\nprint(\"Tokens:\", word_piece_tokens)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.629679Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.630046Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:18.02501Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:17.630019Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:18.024052Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\nOriginal text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\nTokens: ['the', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":112},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Unigram Language Model Tokenization\n\nUnigram tokenization works differently. Instead of starting with individual characters and repeatedly merging them, it starts with a relatively large vocabulary of possible subwords and removes less useful tokens.\n\nUnlike WordPiece, which uses `##` to indicate continuation of a word, Unigram tokenization (SentencePiece) uses `▁` to represent whitespace before a word.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\nunigram_tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    \"google/mt5-small\"\n)\n\nunigram_tokens = unigram_tokenizer.tokenize(text)\n\nprint(\"\\nOriginal text:\", text)\nprint(\"Tokens:\", unigram_tokens)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:18.026099Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:18.026385Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.0808Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:18.026359Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.079831Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\nOriginal text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\nTokens: ['▁The', '▁cat', '▁sat', '▁on', '▁the', '▁mat', '▁', 'because', '▁it', '▁was', '▁', 'tired', '.']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":113},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"It evaluates possible segmentations using probabilities learned from its training data. It selects a segmentation with a high probability according to its model. The vocabulary is gradually reduced by removing tokens that contribute least to the overall likelihood.\n\nWhere is it used?\n\n> SentencePiece supports Unigram and BPE tokenization. Some models, including T5 and several multilingual models, use Unigram-based tokenization.\n\n### Comparison of tokenization algorithms\n\n| Algorithm | Main mechanism                              | Example association     |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| BPE       | Repeatedly merges frequent pairs            | GPT-family tokenizers   |\n| WordPiece | Selects merges using likelihood improvement | BERT                    |\n| Unigram   | Starts large and removes less useful pieces | T5/SentencePiece models |\n\n## Vocabulary\n\nA vocabulary is a collection of all tokens that a tokenizer knows.\n\nImagine a small vocabulary:\n\n| Token | Token ID |\n|---|---:|\n| . | 1 |\n| The | 22 |\n| cat | 24 |\n| mat | 25 |\n| the | 28 |\n| was | 29 |\n| it | 30 |\n| on | 31 |\n| sat | 32 |\n| tired | 34 |\n| because | 50 |","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"Original text:\", text)\nprint(\"Tokens:\", output.tokens)\nprint(\"Token IDs:\", output.ids)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.083304Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.083615Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.089048Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.083588Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.088089Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Original text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\nTokens: ['The', 'cat', 'sat', 'on', 'the', 'mat', 'because', 'it', 'was', 'tired', '.']\nToken IDs: [22, 24, 32, 31, 28, 25, 50, 30, 29, 34, 1]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":114},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"These numbers are called token IDs.\n\nImportant points:\n\n- Each vocabulary token has a corresponding ID.\n- The ID is an identifier, not a measurement of meaning.\n- Token ID 100 is not inherently more meaningful than token ID 10.\n- Different models can assign completely different IDs to the same token.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Display vocabulary tokens with ID\nprint(\"\\nTokens with ID:\")\n\nvocab = tokenizer.get_vocab()\n\nfor token, token_id in sorted(vocab.items(), key=lambda x: x[1]):\n    print(f\"Token ID: {token_id}, Token: {token}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.090177Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.090582Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.108842Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.090555Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.107931Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\nTokens with ID:\nToken ID: 0, Token: [UNK]\nToken ID: 1, Token: .\nToken ID: 2, Token: T\nToken ID: 3, Token: a\nToken ID: 4, Token: b\nToken ID: 5, Token: c\nToken ID: 6, Token: d\nToken ID: 7, Token: e\nToken ID: 8, Token: f\nToken ID: 9, Token: g\nToken ID: 10, Token: h\nToken ID: 11, Token: i\nToken ID: 12, Token: m\nToken ID: 13, Token: n\nToken ID: 14, Token: o\nToken ID: 15, Token: r\nToken ID: 16, Token: s\nToken ID: 17, Token: t\nToken ID: 18, Token: u\nToken ID: 19, Token: w\nToken ID: 20, Token: at\nToken ID: 21, Token: he\nToken ID: 22, Token: The\nToken ID: 23, Token: as\nToken ID: 24, Token: cat\nToken ID: 25, Token: mat\nToken ID: 26, Token: re\nToken ID: 27, Token: ti\nToken ID: 28, Token: the\nToken ID: 29, Token: was\nToken ID: 30, Token: it\nToken ID: 31, Token: on\nToken ID: 32, Token: sat\nToken ID: 33, Token: red\nToken ID: 34, Token: tired\nToken ID: 35, Token: af\nToken ID: 36, Token: au\nToken ID: 37, Token: be\nToken ID: 38, Token: cau\nToken ID: 39, Token: er\nToken ID: 40, Token: ng\nToken ID: 41, Token: se\nToken ID: 42, Token: sit\nToken ID: 43, Token: ter\nToken ID: 44, Token: whe\nToken ID: 45, Token: ting\nToken ID: 46, Token: after\nToken ID: 47, Token: becau\nToken ID: 48, Token: sitting\nToken ID: 49, Token: where\nToken ID: 50, Token: because\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":115},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## How are token IDs converted into embeddings?\n\nThis is where tokenization connects to the Transformer architecture.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"tokenizer","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.11002Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.110861Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.128113Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.110798Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.127182Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":116,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"Tokenizer(version=\"1.0\", truncation=None, padding=None, added_tokens=[{\"id\":0, \"content\":\"[UNK]\", \"single_word\":False, \"lstrip\":False, \"rstrip\":False, \"normalized\":False, \"special\":True}], normalizer=None, pre_tokenizer=Whitespace(), post_processor=None, decoder=None, model=BPE(dropout=None, unk_token=\"[UNK]\", continuing_subword_prefix=None, end_of_word_suffix=None, fuse_unk=False, byte_fallback=False, ignore_merges=False, vocab={\"[UNK]\":0, \".\":1, \"T\":2, \"a\":3, \"b\":4, \"c\":5, \"d\":6, \"e\":7, \"f\":8, \"g\":9, \"h\":10, \"i\":11, \"m\":12, \"n\":13, \"o\":14, \"r\":15, \"s\":16, \"t\":17, \"u\":18, \"w\":19, \"at\":20, \"he\":21, \"The\":22, \"as\":23, \"cat\":24, \"mat\":25, \"re\":26, \"ti\":27, \"the\":28, \"was\":29, \"it\":30, \"on\":31, \"sat\":32, \"red\":33, \"tired\":34, \"af\":35, \"au\":36, \"be\":37, \"cau\":38, \"er\":39, \"ng\":40, \"se\":41, \"sit\":42, \"ter\":43, \"whe\":44, \"ting\":45, \"after\":46, \"becau\":47, \"sitting\":48, \"where\":49, \"because\":50}, merges=[(\"a\", \"t\"), (\"h\", \"e\"), (\"T\", \"he\"), (\"a\", \"s\"), (\"c\", \"at\"), (\"m\", \"at\"), (\"r\", \"e\"), (\"t\", \"i\"), (\"t\", \"he\"), (\"w\", \"as\"), (\"i\", \"t\"), (\"o\", \"n\"), (\"s\", \"at\"), (\"re\", \"d\"), (\"ti\", \"red\"), (\"a\", \"f\"), (\"a\", \"u\"), (\"b\", \"e\"), (\"c\", \"au\"), (\"e\", \"r\"), (\"n\", \"g\"), (\"s\", \"e\"), (\"s\", \"it\"), (\"t\", \"er\"), (\"w\", \"he\"), (\"ti\", \"ng\"), (\"af\", \"ter\"), (\"be\", \"cau\"), (\"sit\", \"ting\"), (\"whe\", \"re\"), (\"becau\", \"se\")]))"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":116},{"cell_type":"code","source":"embedding_dim = 5","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.129244Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.129537Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.150299Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.129509Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.149288Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":117},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Embedding dimension is the number of numerical values used to represent each token (word or subword) in a neural network. It determines how many numbers represent one token.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"vocab_size = tokenizer.get_vocab_size()\nvocab_size","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.151435Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.151809Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.15937Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.151777Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.158281Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":118,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"51"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":118},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Vocabulary size is the total number of unique tokens that a tokenizer knows or can represent. It tells us how many different tokens are available in the tokenizer's vocabulary.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nembedding_layer = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embedding_dim)\n\ntoken_ids = torch.tensor(output.ids, dtype=torch.long)\nembeddings = embedding_layer(token_ids)\n\nprint(f\"\\nEmbedding shape: {embeddings.shape}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.162559Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.163333Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.176652Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.163303Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.175815Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\nEmbedding shape: torch.Size([11, 5])\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":119},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import pandas as pd\n\n# Create embedding table\ndata = embeddings.detach().numpy()\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(\n    data,\n    columns=[f\"Dimension {i+1}\" for i in range(data.shape[1])]\n)\n\n# Add Token ID and Word columns\ndf.insert(0, \"Token ID\", output.ids)\ndf.insert(1, \"Word\", output.tokens)\n\n# Display table\nprint(df.round(2).to_string(index=False))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.177947Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.178306Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.198892Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.17827Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.197952Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":" Token ID    Word  Dimension 1  Dimension 2  Dimension 3  Dimension 4  Dimension 5\n       22     The        -0.00        -0.30        -1.46        -0.10        -0.60\n       24     cat        -0.01         0.24         0.13         0.76         1.10\n       32     sat        -1.90         0.23         0.02        -0.35         0.29\n       31      on         1.60        -2.48        -0.42        -1.20         0.81\n       28     the        -0.38         1.03        -0.69         0.64        -0.97\n       25     mat         0.34         0.72         0.41         1.93         1.01\n       50 because         0.72         0.04         1.31         0.25        -1.98\n       30      it        -0.73         0.10         0.35         0.97        -0.47\n       29     was         0.96         1.62         1.45         0.27        -0.21\n       34   tired         1.29         0.05        -1.55         0.76         0.78\n        1       .        -1.23        -0.04        -1.60        -0.75         1.65\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":120},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The model has an embedding dimension of 5 because the `embedding_dim` is set to 5, for more dimension set it that number.\n\nWhen the model receives token ID `24`, it retrieves the corresponding embedding: `[0.17,        -1.17,        -0.60,        -1.00,        -0.50]`. The corresponding word is `cat`.\n\n## Special tokens\n\nNot every token represents an ordinary word. LLMs also use special tokens to control the structure and flow of conversations.\n\nExamples include:\n\n| Special token | Purpose                                          |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| BOS           | Beginning of sequence                            |\n| EOS           | End of sequence                                  |\n| PAD           | Padding sequences                                |\n| UNK           | Unknown token in some tokenizers                 |\n| SEP           | Separates text segments in some models           |\n| MASK          | Represents masked content in models such as BERT |\n\nSome GPT-style conversational formats use tokens such as:\n\n- `<|im_start|>` — starts a message.\n- `<|im_sep|>` — separates a message header from its content.\n- `<|im_end|>` — ends a message.\n- `<|endoftext|>` — a special end-of-text token in certain GPT tokenizers.\n- `<|ghissue|>` — not a standard token; avoid assuming that all models share the same special-token vocabulary.\n\nFor example, a simplified conversational representation might look like:\n\n```\n<|im_start|>user\nWhat is machine learning?\n<|im_end|>\n\n<|im_start|>assistant\nMachine learning is a branch of AI...\n<|im_end|>\n```\n\nThese markers help a model distinguish between different messages and their boundaries. Actual chat formats vary between model providers.\n\n## Detokenization\n\nDetokenization is the reverse process of tokenization.\n\nTokenization:\n\n```\nHuman-readable text\n       ↓\n     Tokens\n       ↓\n   Token IDs\n```\n\nDetokenization:\n\n```\n   Token IDs\n       ↓\n     Tokens\n       ↓\nHuman-readable text\n```","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Decode token IDs\ndecoded_text = tokenizer.decode(output.ids)\n\nprint(\"Token IDs:\", output.ids)\nprint(\"Decoded text:\", decoded_text)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.200114Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.200571Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.224303Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.200531Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.223293Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Token IDs: [22, 24, 32, 31, 28, 25, 50, 30, 29, 34, 1]\nDecoded text: The cat sat on the mat because it was tired .\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":121},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**One important detail:** some tokenizers operate at the byte level, so decoding must correctly reconstruct UTF-8 text. You cannot always simply join token strings with spaces.\n\n## Relationship between tokenization and context windows\n\nA context window represents the maximum number of tokens a model can process in a given request, subject to the model's specific architecture and API limits.\n\nFor example, suppose a model supports a context window of 8,192 tokens.\n\nYour input contains:\n\n```\nSystem prompt:       500 tokens\nUser question:       200 tokens\nRetrieved RAG data:  6,000 tokens\nConversation history: 1,000 tokens\n```\n\nTotal:\n\n$$ 500+200+6000+1000=7700 $$\n\nYou have:\n\n$$ 8192-7700=492 $$\n\ntokens remaining.\n\nThis is a simplified example; output-token reservations and provider-specific accounting also matter.\n\nWhy should an AI engineer care?\n\nBecause tokenization directly affects:\n\n1. Context-window usage.\n2. API costs.\n3. Response latency.\n4. RAG chunk sizes.\n5. Maximum input and output lengths.\n6. How much conversation history you can retain.\n\n### Tokenization in RAG\n\nImagine you retrieve 10 documents from a vector database. Each document contains 1,000 tokens.\n\nYour retrieved context contains:\n\n$$ 10 \\times 1000 = 10000 \\text{ tokens} $$\n\nIf your model only has 8,192 tokens available for the entire request, you cannot simply insert all 10 documents alongside your instructions and question.\n\nYou need to:\n\n- Reduce the number of retrieved documents.\n- Use smaller chunks.\n- Rerank the retrieved chunks.\n- Remove redundant content.\n- Compress or summarize retrieved information.\n\nThis is why tokenization is important when designing RAG pipelines.\n\n## Why does the same sentence produce different token counts?\n\nDifferent LLMs can use different tokenizers and vocabularies.\n\nConsider: `Artificial Intelligence`\n\n- One tokenizer might produce: `[\"Artificial\", \" Intelligence\"]`\n- Another might produce: `[\"Art\", \"ificial\", \" Intelligence\"]`\n\nBoth are valid tokenization strategies.\n\nOther factors that affect token counts include:\n\n- Language.\n- Special characters.\n- Emojis.\n- Numbers.\n- Programming code.\n- Whitespace.\n- Vocabulary and tokenizer design.\n\nFor example, Bangla text can have a different token-to-character ratio from English text.\n\nThis is important when building multilingual applications.\n\n## Misconceptions\n\n| Misconception                               | Correct understanding                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| One token equals one word                   | A token can be a word, subword, character, or byte sequence.                            |\n| Token IDs represent meaning                 | Token IDs are identifiers, not semantic values.                                         |\n| Tokenization understands language           | Tokenization segments text; semantic representations are learned by the neural network. |\n| All LLMs use the same tokenizer             | Different models can use different tokenizers.                                          |\n| Tokenization and embedding are the same     | Tokenization creates tokens; embedding maps token IDs into vectors.                     |\n| A shorter sentence always uses fewer tokens | Token count depends on the tokenizer and text composition.                              |\n\n## Interview questions\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is tokenization?</summary>\n\nTokenization is the process of converting raw text into smaller units called tokens. These tokens are then mapped to numerical IDs, which an LLM uses as input.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do modern LLMs use subword tokenization?</summary>\n\nSubword tokenization balances vocabulary size and sequence length. It allows models to represent unfamiliar words by combining smaller known pieces instead of requiring every possible word in the vocabulary.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is the difference between BPE and WordPiece?</summary>\n\nBPE repeatedly merges frequent adjacent token pairs, whereas WordPiece generally selects vocabulary merges based on their improvement to the likelihood of the training data.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is the difference between tokenization and embedding?</summary>\n\nTokenization converts text into tokens and token IDs. Embedding converts token IDs into dense numerical vectors that a neural network can process.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What happens when a model encounters an unknown word?</summary>\n\nA subword or byte-level tokenizer can often represent the unfamiliar word using smaller known units. Therefore, it doesn't necessarily need an unknown-token representation.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>How does tokenization affect LLM API costs?</summary>\n\nMany LLM APIs calculate input and output charges based on token counts. More tokens generally mean higher costs, although exact pricing depends on the provider and model.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why is tokenization important in RAG?</summary>\n\nIt helps control the size of retrieved context, chunk sizes, context-window consumption, and API costs.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is detokenization?</summary>\n\nDetokenization converts token IDs back into human-readable text.\n\n</details>\n\n# Positional Encoding\n\nPositional Encoding is a technique used in Transformer architectures to give the model information about the position or order of tokens in a sequence.\n\n## Why do we need positional encoding?\n\nConsider these two sentences:\n\n- Sentence A: `The cat ate the mouse.`\n- Sentence B: `The mouse ate the cat.`\n\nBoth sentences contain almost exactly the same words, but their meanings are completely different. The difference comes from the order of the words.\n\nNow, consider how a Transformer processes text. First, it converts tokens into numerical vectors using an embedding layer.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Token | Token embedding         |\n| ----- | ----------------------- |\n| The   | [0.21, 0.45, 0.12, ...] |\n| cat   | [0.32, 0.18, 0.67, ...] |\n| ate   | [0.51, 0.22, 0.39, ...] |\n| mouse | [0.14, 0.71, 0.28, ...] |\n\nThe problem is that self-attention, by itself, does not inherently know the original order of these tokens. Self-attention can determine relationships between tokens, but without positional information, it does not know whether `cat` appeared before or after `ate`. This is why we introduce positional encoding. It adds positional information to token embeddings so the Transformer can understand the sequence order.\n\n## Where does positional encoding happen in a Transformer?\n\nThe process is:\n\n1. User provides text.\n2. Tokenizer converts text into token IDs.\n3. Embedding layer converts token IDs into vectors.\n4. **Positional encoding** generates position vectors.\n5. **Token embeddings and positional encodings are added together.**\n6. The resulting vectors are passed into the Transformer layers.\n\nThe mathematical operation is:\n\n$$ X = E + PE $$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $X$ = final input representation.\n- $E$ = token embedding.\n- $PE$ = positional encoding.\n\nFor example:\n\nSuppose we have a token embedding:\n\n$$ E\\_{\\text{cat}}=[0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8] $$\n\nAnd its positional encoding:\n\n$$ PE_1=[0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7] $$\n\nThen:\n\n$$ X\\_{\\text{cat}} = [0.3,0.7,1.1,1.5] $$\n\nThis final vector contains both:\n\n- Semantic information: What does the token mean?\n- Positional information: Where does the token appear?\n\nAn important point: positional encoding does not replace token embeddings. It adds information to them.\n\n## Internal Working\n\nIn the original Transformer architecture ([Attention Is All You Need, 2017](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2017/file/3f5ee243547dee91fbd053c1c4a845aa-Paper.pdf)), the authors introduced Sinusoidal Positional Encoding.\n\nIt uses mathematical functions called:\n\n- Sine\n- Cosine\n\nThese functions generate unique position-dependent patterns.\n\nThe formulas are:\n\nFor even dimensions:\n\n$$ PE(pos,2i)= \\sin\\left( \\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d\\_{model}}} \\right) $$\n\nFor odd dimensions:\n\n$$ PE(pos,2i+1)= \\cos\\left( \\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d\\_{model}}} \\right) $$\n\nLet's understand every variable.\n\n| Variable         | Meaning                                       |\n| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| $pos$        | Position of the token in the sequence         |\n| $i$          | Dimension index pair                          |\n| $d\\_{model}$ | Embedding dimension                           |\n| $2i$         | Even dimension                                |\n| $2i+1$       | Odd dimension                                 |\n| 10000            | Constant controlling the range of frequencies |\n\n### Why are there different frequencies?\n\nBecause the Transformer needs positional patterns at different scales.\n\n- Higher-frequency dimensions change more quickly between positions.\n- Lower-frequency dimensions change more slowly.\n\nThis creates a combination of patterns that helps the Transformer distinguish different token positions.\n\n\nLook at this part:\n\n$$\n10000^{2i/d_{model}}\n$$\n\nThis is the denominator that controls the frequency.\n\nFor example, suppose:\n\n$$\nd_{model}=8\n$$\n\nWe have four pairs of sine and cosine dimensions.\n\n| i | Denominator | Frequency |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 0 | 1 | Highest |\n| 1 | 10 | Lower |\n| 2 | 100 | Lower |\n| 3 | 1000 | Lowest |\n\nNotice what happens:\n\n- Dividing by 1 makes the wave change quickly.\n- Dividing by 10 makes it change more slowly.\n- Dividing by 100 makes it even slower.\n- Dividing by 1000 makes it very slow.\n\nThis is how different dimensions capture different positional scales.\n\n\n**Important:** When the embedding dimension is odd, the final dimension has no cosine partner. This is perfectly valid in the original Transformer positional encoding method.\n\nThese vectors represent the positions, not the meanings of the words.\n\n## Sine and Cosine\n\nThis is an important theoretical question. Why didn't the original Transformer use only sine? There are three main reasons.\n\n### Different patterns\n\nSine and cosine are phase-shifted versions of each other.\n\n$$ \\cos(x)=\\sin(x+\\pi/2) $$\n\nUsing both provides two complementary values for each frequency.\n\n### Relative position information\n\nThe sinusoidal functions have a useful mathematical property.\n\nFor a given frequency $\\omega$:\n\n$$ \\sin((pos+k)\\omega) $$\n\nand\n\n$$ \\cos((pos+k)\\omega) $$\n\ncan be expressed using the sine and cosine values at $pos$.\n\nUsing angle addition:\n\n$$ \\sin(a+b)=\\sin(a)\\cos(b)+\\cos(a)\\sin(b) $$\n\n$$ \\cos(a+b)=\\cos(a)\\cos(b)-\\sin(a)\\sin(b) $$\n\nThis means a fixed positional offset can be represented through a transformation of the original positional values. It helps the model learn relationships between relative positions.\n\nFor example:\n\n- cat is 1 position after The.\n- sat is 1 position after cat.\n- sat is 2 positions after The.\n\n### Different frequencies\n\n- Different dimensions encode positional patterns at different scales.\n- Some dimensions change rapidly, while others change slowly.\n- Combining them helps represent both short-range and long-range positional information.\n\n## Types of positional Encoding\n\nSinusoidal positional encoding is not the only approach. There are several important approaches used in Transformer architectures.\n\n### Sinusoidal positional encoding\n\nUses fixed sine and cosine mathematical functions.\n\n- Original Transformer approach.\n- No trainable positional parameters.\n- Position vectors are calculated mathematically.\n\n![Sinusoidal Positional Encoding](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/sinusoidal_positional_encoding.png)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"max_len = embeddings.shape[0]","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.225445Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.225949Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.248449Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.225908Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.247682Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":122},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Create an empty positional encoding matrix\npe = torch.zeros(max_len, embedding_dim)\nprint(pe.shape)\nprint(pe)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.249533Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.250272Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.274503Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.250243Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:26:19.27364Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"torch.Size([11, 5])\ntensor([[0., 0., 0., 0.