e":"stdout","text":"Attention weights shape: torch.Size([1, 11, 11])\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":147},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"- The first row represents the word I.\n- `0.1489` means I pays `14.89%` attention to itself.\n- `0.2310` means I pays `23.10%` attention to love.\n- `0.2337` means I pays `23.37%` attention to the period.\n\n## Multiply Attention Weights with Value\n\nWe have now calculated how much attention each token should receive. But we still need to retrieve the actual information. That's why we use Value vectors.\n\nThe final attention output is calculated as:\n\n$$ Attention(Q,K,V)= Softmax\\left( \\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}} \\right)V $$\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n```\nQuery × Key Transpose\n          |\n          v\n    Attention Scores\n          |\n          v\n        Scaling\n          |\n          v\n        Softmax\n          |\n          v\n   Attention Weights\n          |\n          v\n     Multiply by V\n          |\n          v\n   Final Attention Output\n```","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Calculate self-attention output\nattention_output = torch.matmul(\n    attention_weights,\n    V\n)\n\nprint(\"Attention output shape:\", attention_output.shape)\nprint(\"Attention output:\")\nprint(attention_output)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:27:01.789627Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:27:01.790031Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:27:01.79889Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:27:01.79Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:27:01.797881Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Attention output shape: torch.Size([1, 11, 5])\nAttention output:\ntensor([[[ 0.4939, -0.0993, -0.8119,  0.5649, -0.5746],\n         [ 0.6005, -0.1050, -0.6068,  0.7148, -0.7105],\n         [ 0.5157, -0.0798, -0.7539,  0.6399, -0.6620],\n         [ 0.4276, -0.0851, -0.9375,  0.3150, -0.4590],\n         [ 0.5202, -0.0894, -0.7264,  0.6284, -0.6287],\n         [ 0.6362, -0.1025, -0.4916,  0.7876, -0.7625],\n         [ 0.5839, -0.0751, -0.6025,  0.7424, -0.7557],\n         [ 0.5904, -0.0910, -0.5933,  0.7269, -0.7239],\n         [ 0.6364, -0.0929, -0.5393,  0.7997, -0.7935],\n         [ 0.5014, -0.1093, -0.7974,  0.4457, -0.5110],\n         [ 0.4527, -0.0876, -0.8834,  0.5298, -0.5598]]],\n       grad_fn=<UnsafeViewBackward0>)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":148},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Notice something important:\n\nThe original representation of `cat` has now been transformed by combining information from other tokens.\n\nThat's the main purpose of self-attention.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"class SelfAttention(nn.Module):\n\n    def __init__(self, d_model):\n        super().__init__()\n\n        self.d_model = d_model\n\n        # Linear projections\n        self.W_q = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.W_k = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n        self.W_v = nn.Linear(d_model, d_model)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n\n        # Generate Query, Key, and Value\n        Q = self.W_q(x)\n        K = self.W_k(x)\n        V = self.W_v(x)\n\n        # Calculate attention scores\n        scores = torch.matmul(\n            Q,\n            K.transpose(-2, -1)\n        )\n\n        # Scale attention scores\n        scores = scores / math.sqrt(self.d_model)\n\n        # Apply softmax\n        attention_weights = torch.softmax(\n            scores,\n            dim=-1\n        )\n\n        # Calculate attention output\n        output = torch.matmul(\n            attention_weights,\n            V\n        )\n\n        return output, attention_weights","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:27:04.254048Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:27:04.254393Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:27:04.261778Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:27:04.254365Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:27:04.260772Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":149},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Initialize self-attention\nself_attention = SelfAttention(d_model=5)\n\n# Apply self-attention to final embeddings\nattention_output, attention_weights = self_attention(\n    final_embeddings\n)\n\nprint(\"Attention Output:\")\nprint(attention_output)\n\nprint(\"Attention Weights:\")\nprint(attention_weights)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-07T17:27:06.794158Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-07T17:27:06.794552Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-07T17:27:06.806165Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-07T17:27:06.79452Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-07T17:27:06.80514Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Attention Output:\ntensor([[[ 0.1297,  0.8666,  0.1965, -0.4702,  0.4514],\n         [ 0.0940,  0.5612, -0.0751, -0.3179,  0.4571],\n         [ 0.0674,  0.3730, -0.1724, -0.3188,  0.6525],\n         [ 0.0190,  0.3129, -0.1092, -0.3360,  0.7205],\n         [ 0.0709,  0.5220, -0.0357, -0.4232,  0.6676],\n         [ 0.1281,  0.3809, -0.1989, -0.3039,  0.6447],\n         [ 0.1322,  0.9426,  0.1598, -0.5011,  0.4925],\n         [ 0.0898,  0.5902, -0.0479, -0.3923,  0.5729],\n         [ 0.1281,  0.9209,  0.1250, -0.4261,  0.3539],\n         [ 0.0865,  0.6425,  0.0839, -0.4027,  0.5052],\n         [ 0.0680,  0.2252, -0.2444, -0.2628,  0.7130]]],\n       grad_fn=<UnsafeViewBackward0>)\nAttention Weights:\ntensor([[[0.0614, 0.0906, 0.0631, 0.2934, 0.0360, 0.0438, 0.0467, 0.0479,\n          0.0986, 0.1395, 0.0790],\n         [0.0826, 0.1013, 0.0937, 0.0890, 0.0826, 0.1082, 0.0671, 0.0853,\n          0.0805, 0.0950, 0.1147],\n         [0.0836, 0.1039, 0.0827, 0.0577, 0.0858, 0.1121, 0.1036, 0.1023,\n          0.1192, 0.0797, 0.0694],\n         [0.0915, 0.0553, 0.0898, 0.1209, 0.0815, 0.0348, 0.2027, 0.0812,\n          0.1388, 0.0517, 0.0518],\n         [0.0645, 0.1271, 0.0631, 0.1371, 0.0432, 0.0800, 0.0674, 0.0715,\n          0.1581, 0.1283, 0.0597],\n         [0.0668, 0.1244, 0.0808, 0.0287, 0.0806, 0.1905, 0.0727, 0.1066,\n          0.1034, 0.0657, 0.0798],\n         [0.0566, 0.0979, 0.0387, 0.2927, 0.0286, 0.0462, 0.0291, 0.0410,\n          0.0810, 0.2359, 0.0523],\n         [0.0713, 0.1246, 0.0672, 0.1258, 0.0534, 0.0965, 0.0560, 0.0741,\n          0.1121, 0.1423, 0.0767],\n         [0.0736, 0.0863, 0.0592, 0.2412, 0.0509, 0.0609, 0.0302, 0.0493,\n          0.0491, 0.1970, 0.1025],\n         [0.0736, 0.0815, 0.0907, 0.2060, 0.0542, 0.0466, 0.0891, 0.0644,\n          0.1205, 0.0844, 0.0890],\n         [0.0769, 0.0776, 0.0996, 0.0210, 0.1127, 0.1210, 0.1602, 0.1209,\n          0.1136, 0.0316, 0.0650]]], grad_fn=<SoftmaxBackward0>)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":150},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Complete Encoder Self-Attention architecture\n\n![Self Attention in Encoder](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/self_attention_in_encoder.png)\n\nThe complete operation:\n\nStep 1: Receive input embeddings with positional information.\n\nStep 2: Generate three matrices:\n\n$$ Q=XW_Q,\\quad K=XW_K,\\quad V=XW_V $$\n\nStep 3: Calculate similarity scores:\n\n$$ S=QK^T $$\n\nStep 4: Scale scores:\n\n$$ S'=\\frac{S}{\\sqrt{d_k}} $$\n\nStep 5: Apply Softmax:\n\n$$ A=Softmax(S') $$\n\nStep 6: Multiply attention weights by Values:\n\n$$ Output=AV $$\n\nThe output contains contextual representations for all input tokens.\n\n## Multi-Head Self-Attention\n\nSo far, we have discussed a single attention head.\n\nHowever, the original Transformer uses Multi-Head Attention.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause different attention heads can learn different relationships between tokens.\n\nFor example, consider:\n\n> The cat sat on the mat because it was tired.\n\nDifferent heads might learn to focus on:\n\n- Head 1: Relationships between nouns and pronouns.\n- Head 2: Relationships between verbs and subjects.\n- Head 3: Relationships between nearby words.\n- Head 4: Longer-distance contextual relationships.\n\nThese are possible learned patterns, not guaranteed fixed roles.\n\n![Multi Head Attention](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/multi_head_attention.png)\n\nThe process:\n\n1. Generate Query, Key, and Value projections for each head.\n2. Each head independently calculates attention.\n3. Each head produces its own output.\n4. Concatenate all head outputs.\n5. Pass the concatenated result through a final linear projection.\n\nMathematically:\n\n$$ head_i=Attention(Q_i,K_i,V_i) $$\n\nThen:\n\n$$ MultiHead(Q,K,V)= Concat(head_1,\\ldots,head_h)W_O $$\n\nHere:\n\n- $h$ = number of attention heads.\n- $W_O$ = output projection matrix.\n\nFor example, if the model dimension is 512 and there are 8 heads:\n\n$$ d_k=d_v=\\frac{512}{8}=64 $$\n\nEach head operates on 64-dimensional Query, Key, and Value vectors.\n\nThe eight outputs are concatenated:\n\n$$ 8 \\times 64=512 $$\n\nThe resulting representation has 512 dimensions again.\n\n## What happens after Encoder Self-Attention?\n\nSelf-attention does not finish the encoder layer. Its output moves into the Add & Normalize operation.\n\nThe original Transformer uses a residual connection:\n\n$$ Y=LayerNorm(X+Attention(X)) $$\n\nHere:\n\n- $X$ is the original input.\n- $Attention(X)$ is the attention output.\n- LayerNorm normalizes the combined result.\n\nThen the result moves into the Feed-Forward Neural Network (FFN).\n\n$$ FFN(x)=W_2\\sigma(W_1x+b_1)+b_2 $$\n\nThe FFN processes each token's representation independently, applying learned nonlinear transformations.\n\nAfter another residual connection and normalization, the encoder layer is complete.\n\nThis output can then be passed to the next encoder layer.\n\n## Interview questions\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Does every token attend to every other token?</summary>\n\nYes, in standard encoder self-attention.\n\nConsider:\n\n```\nThe cat sat on the mat\n```\n\nThe attention matrix looks conceptually like this:\n\nIllustrative attention matrix\n\nRows = Query tokens · Columns = Key tokens\n\n|     | The | cat | sat | on  | the | mat |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| The | 0.2 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.1 |\n| cat | 0.1 | 0.5 | 0.2 | 0.0 | 0.1 | 0.1 |\n| sat | 0.1 | 0.3 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 |\n| on  | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.3 | 0.1 | 0.2 |\n| the | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.4 |\n| mat | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.2 | 0.1 | 0.2 | 0.2 |\n\nNumbers are illustrative, not actual model outputs.\n\nEach row has its own attention distribution.\n\nThis is called bidirectional self-attention because a token can attend to tokens appearing both before and after it.\n\nFor example, `cat` can attend to `sat`, even though `sat` comes after it.\n\nThis is different from decoder causal self-attention, where future tokens are masked.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is encoder self-attention?</summary>\n\nEncoder self-attention is a mechanism that allows each input token to calculate its relevance to every other token in the input sequence. It uses Query, Key, and Value projections to generate contextual representations.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do we need Query, Key, and Value?</summary>\n\nQuery represents what information a token is looking for, Key represents the information used for matching, and Value represents the information that gets aggregated based on attention weights.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do we use Softmax?</summary>\n\nSoftmax converts attention scores into normalized weights, allowing the model to calculate a weighted combination of Value vectors.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do we divide by the square root of the Key dimension?</summary>\n\nTo prevent dot-product scores from becoming excessively large, which can saturate Softmax and produce very small gradients.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Can an encoder token attend to future tokens?</summary>\n\nYes. Standard encoder self-attention is bidirectional, meaning each token can attend to both previous and subsequent tokens.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>What is the difference between self-attention and cross-attention?</summary>\n\n- Self-attention: Q, K, and V originate from the same sequence.\n- Cross-attention: Q originates from one sequence, while K and V originate from another sequence.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do we need Multi-Head Attention?</summary>\n\nIt allows the model to learn different types of relationships between tokens simultaneously through multiple independent attention heads.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Why do we multiply attention weights by V?</summary>\n\nTo combine information from relevant tokens\n\nAttention weights determine how much information each Value contributes to the output.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n\n<summary>Can encoder self-attention look at future tokens?</summary>\n\nYes, in standard bidirectional encoder attention\n\nEncoder self-attention is bidirectional. Causal masking is used in autoregressive decoder self-attention.\n\n</details>","metadata":{"jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed":true}}]}